    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We start with the polynomial equation \\(z^{28} - z^8 - 1 = 0\\) and use the fact that \\(|z| = 1\\). Since \\(z\\) is a complex number with magnitude 1, we can write \\(z = e^{i\\theta}\\) for some \\(\\theta\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(z = e^{i\\theta}\\) into the polynomial equation, we get:\n\\[\n(e^{i\\theta})^{28} - (e^{i\\theta})^8 - 1 = 0\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\ne^{28i\\theta} - e^{8i\\theta} - 1 = 0\n\\]\nMultiplying through by \\(e^{-8i\\theta}\\), we get:\n\\[\ne^{16i\\theta} - 1 - e^{-8i\\theta} = 0\n\\]\nUsing Euler's formula again, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos 16\\theta - 1 - \\cos(-8\\theta) = 0\n\\]\nSince \\(\\cos(-8\\theta) = \\cos 8\\theta\\), the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\cos 16\\theta - \\cos 8\\theta = 1\n\\]\nUsing the cosine subtraction formula \\(\\cos A - \\cos B = -2 \\sin((A+B)/2) \\sin((A-B)/2)\\), we set \\(A = 16\\theta\\) and \\(B = 8\\theta\\), so:\n\\[\n\\cos 16\\theta - \\cos 8\\theta = -2 \\sin(14\\theta) \\sin(8\\theta)\n\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n-2 \\sin(14\\theta) \\sin(8\\theta) = 1\n\\]\nSolving for \\(\\sin(14\\theta)\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\sin(14\\theta) = -\\frac{1}{2 \\sin(8\\theta)}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sin(14\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(8\\theta)\\) are both between -1 and 1, we need to find values of \\(\\theta\\) such that the equation holds. The solutions to \\(\\sin(14\\theta) = -\\frac{1}{2 \\sin(8\\theta)}\\) are simple to identify. One such solution is \\(\\theta = 18\\). Substituting \\(\\theta = 18\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\sin(14 \\cdot 18) = -\\frac{1}{2 \\sin(8 \\cdot 18)} \\implies \\sin(252) = -\\frac{1}{2 \\sin(144)}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sin(252) = \\sin(18)\\) and \\(\\sin(144) = \\sin(252/3) = \\sin(18)\\), the equation holds true.\n\nThus, \\(\\theta = 18\\) is a solution. Since we are dealing with a polynomial equation where the roots are symmetrically distributed around \\(360^\\circ\\), the angles \\(\\theta\\) must be of the form \\(360k + 18\\) for integer \\(k\\). The only other solution within the interval \\(0 \\leq \\theta < 360\\) is \\(\\theta = 150^\\circ\\), which gives \\(\\theta = 18 + 120\\).\n\nTherefore, \\(\\theta_2 + \\theta_4 + \\dots + \\theta_{2n} = 18 + 150 + 222 + \\dots + (2n-12) = 990\\). The value of \\(\\theta_2 + \\theta_4 + \\dots + \\theta_{2n}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{990}\n\\]",
