    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for the complex numbers \\( z \\) that satisfy both \\( z^{28} - z^{8} - 1 = 0 \\) and \\( |z| = 1 \\), we express \\( z \\) in polar form as \\( z = e^{i\\theta} \\). The equation \\( |z| = 1 \\) is automatically satisfied, so we need to find the values of \\( \\theta \\) that satisfy the polynomial equation in \\( z^{28} \\) and \\( z^{8} \\).\n\nRewriting the polynomial equation using \\( z = e^{i\\theta} \\), we get:\n\\[ z^{28} - z^8 - 1 = 0. \\]\nLet \\( w = z^7 \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[ w^4 - w - 1 = 0. \\]\nTo find the roots of \\( w^4 - w - 1 = 0 \\) in the complex plane, we can use numerical methods or recognize that it has roots involving the primitive 21st roots of unity. The solutions to \\( w^4 - w - 1 = 0 \\) correspond to the angles \\( 7\\theta \\) where \\( \\theta \\) is a root of the equation \\( e^{7\\theta} = 1 + \\sqrt{3}i \\).\n\nThe roots of \\( e^{7\\theta} = 1 + \\sqrt{3}i \\) are:\n\\[ e^{7\\theta} = 2 \\left( \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{3} + i \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = 2e^{i\\pi/3}. \\]\nSo, \\( \\theta = \\frac{\\pi/3 + 2k\\pi}{7} \\) for integers \\( k \\). The solutions within the range \\( 0 < \\theta < 288^\\circ \\) (since \\( \\theta + 2k \\leq 288^\\circ \\)) are:\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{10\\pi}{21}, \\frac{17\\pi}{21}, \\frac{24\\pi}{21}, \\frac{31\\pi}{21}. \\]\nThese correspond to the angles \\( 50^\\circ, 173^\\circ, 304^\\circ, 456^\\circ \\) (mod \\( 360^\\circ \\)). Therefore, the angles in the form \\( \\theta_m \\) are:\n\\[ \\theta_1 = \\frac{50\\pi}{180} = \\frac{5\\pi}{18}, \\quad \\theta_2 = \\frac{173\\pi}{180}, \\quad \\theta_3 = \\frac{382\\pi}{180} = \\frac{191\\pi}{90}, \\quad \\theta_4 = \\frac{456\\pi}{180} = \\frac{17\\pi}{12} = 120^\\circ + \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 360^\\circ = 240^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_5 = \\frac{517\\pi}{180} = \\frac{23\\pi}{15}, \\quad \\theta_6 = \\frac{616\\pi}{180} = \\frac{47\\pi}{45}, \\quad \\theta_7 = \\frac{719\\pi}{180} = \\frac{32\\pi}{9} = 384^\\circ + 56^\\circ = 440^\\circ. \\]\n\nThe angles \\( \\theta_2, \\theta_4, \\theta_6 \\) are \\( \\frac{173\\pi}{180} \\), \\( 240^\\circ \\), and \\( \\frac{47\\pi}{45} \\) respectively. To find \\( \\theta_2 + \\theta_4 + \\theta_6 \\):\n\\[ \\theta_2 + \\theta_4 + \\theta_6 = \\frac{173\\pi}{180} + \\frac{17\\pi}{12} + \\frac{47\\pi}{45} = \\frac{173\\pi}{180} + \\frac{255\\pi}{180} + \\frac{188\\pi}{180} = \\frac{616\\pi}{180} = \\frac{154\\pi}{45}. \\]\nConverting \\( \\frac{154\\pi}{45} \\) to degrees, we get:\n\\[ \\frac{154\\pi}{45} \\cdot \\frac{180}{\\pi} = 680^\\circ. \\]\nTherefore, the value of \\( \\theta_2 + \\theta_4 + \\theta_6 \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{680}. \\]",
